\"\nsupervisor/tests/test_runbooks.py:84:        \"---\\ntask_id: R0001\\nsequence: 1\\ntitle: Asset work\\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\\nplaywright_spec:\\nasset_impact: required\\nasset_ids:\\nsource_specifications: specification/04-experience-contract.md#gate\\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-WORLD-001\\nauthoring_batch: B0001\\nfactory_stages: F004,F012,F013\\n---\\n\"\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '30,75p' projects/README.md; sed -n '35,65p' runbooks/README.md; sed -n '1,120p' runbooks/F015.md; sed -n '1,90p' runbooks/F013.md; jq '.batches[] | select(.id==\\\"B0006\\\")' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json; jq 'keys' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n  \u2192 repository F001\u2013F016 factory files (with --project <project-name>)\n  \u2192 projects/<project-name>/authoring-runbooks/B....md\n  \u2192 projects/<project-name>/runbooks/R....md\n```\n\nThe F-series files are reusable source factory instructions. They create the\nproject folder, specification, complete work checklist, and first B-series\nfiles. A B-series file is a *runbook writer*: it takes one small part of that\nchecklist and writes no more than seven R-series Markdown files. \u201cBounded\u201d is\nonly that size limit. It does not build the application. An R-series file is a\n*product-work instruction*: it contains the detailed steps and success evidence\nfor one small piece of real product work.\n\nIn other words, the step that creates the R files is not an F-series file:\n`F014` creates the first B files, then running each B file creates its R files.\n\nFor a very large product, a B dispatcher file creates more B-series runbook\nwriters for the remaining areas of the checklist. This is how the collection\nscales to hundreds or thousands of R-series files without oversized prompts or\na separate manual run. The parent collection explicitly registers its generated\nchild collections, so the originating `--run-all` invocation follows them\nautomatically.\n\n`supervisor-run --project <project-name>` is the normal way to start or resume\none project. It reads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs the factory source\ncollection, follows only child collections belonging to that workspace, and\nstores factory task state in `projects/<project-name>/.supervisor/factory.sqlite3`.\nAccepted tasks are skipped on later invocations; the first unfinished task is\nresumed using its existing collection state.\nagent retrieve focused canonical context without treating procedural F/B files\nas its requirements source.\n\nFor example, a game may eventually have R runbooks for story, combat, content,\nsaves, and platform delivery; an IDE may have R runbooks for language services,\nediting, debugging, and extensions. The F-series derives those areas from the\nbrief rather than assuming either example.\n\n`F001`\u2013`F004` understand the brief and domains, `F005`\u2013`F010` make the delivery\nmap, `F011`\u2013`F013` create and check the specification and work checklist, and\n`F014` creates the first B-series runbook-writing files. When Supervisor runs\nthose B files, each one writes up to seven R-series product-work runbooks. If\nmore R files are required, B dispatcher files create more B files. `F015`\u2013`F016`\nestablish quality and handoff material.\n\n## Run the collection\n\nFrom the repository root, create a named project brief with `supervisor initial`,\nreview `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md`, then run:\n\n```zsh\nsupervisor-run --project <project-name>\n```\n\n`--run-initial` runs only `F001`, which is useful for checking that the named\nbrief can be normalized before committing to the full factory. `--project`\nreads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs every F-series factory stage, then\ndynamically follows its registered B-series and R-series collections. It keeps\nstate inside the named workspace and stops only for a real failure or explicit\nreview gate.\n\n---\ntask_id: F015\nsequence: 15\ntitle: Validate generated authoring batches and implementation contracts\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nCreate `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-generation-quality-gate.md`\nand validation instructions/scripts that can be run after every B-series wave.\nValidate the authoring collection itself and the R-series contracts it produces.\nThe validation is structural and traceability-based; it must not claim the\nproduct has been implemented or released.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Validation checks required front matter, unique IDs, sequence ordering,\n  requirement links, dependency closure, output ownership, and unsupported\n  circular dependencies. Every R contract must also have non-empty provenance:\n  canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, its originating\n  `authoring_batch`, and valid `factory_stages`; the links must resolve to the\n  manifest and canonical specification rather than merely naming an F or B\n  procedure file.\n- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit asset assessment:\n  `asset_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`, `asset_ids` is present,\n  asset-required contracts reserve non-empty unique stable IDs and an asset\n  brief/style version, and non-asset contracts do not silently introduce asset\n  work. It reports missing, duplicate, ambiguous, or unowned asset IDs.\n- It detects duplicate browser test paths and confirms browser-impact metadata\n  only where browser behaviour is actually in scope.\n- It reports catalogue/manifest/specification coverage counts and identifies\n  every missing, duplicate, blocked, or unverified contract by ID.\n- It fails the quality gate when a discovered domain family has no specification\n  chapter, content/authoring approach where applicable, implementation\n  contracts, or traceable verification. It checks category-appropriate\n  completeness rather than applying a fixed checklist to every product.\n- It includes recovery: how to rerun one failed B-series authoring task, repair\n  a bounded R-series range, and resume the next dispatcher wave without\n  regenerating accepted work.\n- Human gates, paid services, credentials, legal/compliance decisions, device\n  access, and store/certification actions remain explicit review points.\n---\ntask_id: F013\nsequence: 13\ntitle: Plan scalable runbook-authoring batches\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nCreate `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json` and\nits readable Markdown companion. It is a persistent allocation ledger, not a\none-shot list: partition the currently available `IMP-*` catalogue into ordered,\nsmall authoring batches and retain queued domain-expansion work. Each batch\nwill later be a normal Supervisor runbook that writes a bounded set of final\nimplementation runbooks.\n\nUse IDs `B0001`, `B0002`, \u2026 for authoring batches and reserve IDs `R0001`,\n`R0002`, \u2026 for generated implementation runbooks. Limit a batch to at most 7\nR-series contracts and reduce that limit when context, coupling, or a platform\ndomain requires it. This is the scaling mechanism: a thousand implementation\nrunbooks become many independent, resumable authoring tasks.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Every available `IMP-*` record belongs to exactly one batch or has a\n  documented human gate/deferment; queued requirements identify their future\n  expansion chapter. Every allocated `R-*` ID is unique and immutable.\n- Batch ordering respects the catalogue dependency graph and identifies batches\n  safe to author in parallel versus those requiring a preceding decision.\n- Each batch has a context packet listing only the relevant specification,\n  catalogue records, terminology, target constraints, required templates, and\n  explicit provenance values for every allocated R contract: canonical\n  `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, originating\n  `authoring_batch`, and the relevant `factory_stages`.\n- No authoring batch is assigned more than seven R-series contracts; a smaller\n  batch is required when its contracts are tightly coupled or context-heavy.\n- The manifest records expected runbook count, IDs, dependencies, output paths,\n  acceptance checks, checkpoint cadence, recovery instructions, and an explicit\n  asset assessment for every allocated R contract. The assessment records\n  `asset_impact` as either `required` or `not_applicable`; asset-required\n  contracts reserve stable, product-specific `asset_ids` and identify their\n  asset brief/purpose before a B-series writer is assigned.\n- Batches preserve end-to-end vertical slices as well as foundation work, so a\n  large product can be implemented and reviewed incrementally.\n- The ledger contains monotonic next-ID counters and wave state, so a resumed\n  dispatcher never renumbers accepted B-series or R-series work.\n{\n  \"id\": \"B0006\",\n  \"order\": 6,\n  \"title\": \"Foundation documentation closure\",\n  \"state\": \"planned\",\n  \"depends_on_batches\": [\n    \"B0004\",\n    \"B0003\"\n  ],\n  \"parallel_with\": [\n    \"B0005 after B0004 acceptance\"\n  ],\n  \"preceding_decision\": \"R0006 must be accepted or documented as blocked; no gate is implicitly resolved\",\n  \"r_contract_ids\": [\n    \"R0012\"\n  ],\n  \"context_packet\": {\n    \"specification\": [\n      \"specification/03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability\",\n      \"specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics\",\n      \"specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\"\n    ],\n    \"catalogue_records\": [\n      \"IMP-0012\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"checkpoint\": \"cross-check all foundation ownership, dependencies, and gates before accepting the wave\",\n  \"recovery\": \"retain R0012 and correct only its documentation contrac